title: Doing things again
card: Chapter 4 | Doing things again | Repeat, for each, as long as, every and whenever
say: Computers are good at doing things again and again. Fathom has a few ways to say how many times.
uncard

new: loops.fathom
type:
  genesis:
    repeat 3 times:
      scrawl "Hip hip hooray!"
    for each n from 1 to 5:
      scrawl n, " times 7 is ", n times 7
  and the universe said i love you
zoom: lines 2-5
spot: lines 2-3
say: Repeat three times does the lines below it three times.
spot: lines 4-5
say:
  For each n from one to five does them once for each number, and while it does, the name n holds that number.
  So the first time round n is one, then two, and so on up to five.
spot: off
run
zoom: output
say: Three cheers, and the seven times table.
wait
zoom: out

say: For each can count in steps, too, and even count down.
line: 5
type:

    for each n from 10 to 0 by -5:
      scrawl n
zoom: lines 6-7
say: From ten to zero, by minus five: ten, five, zero.
zoom: out

say: When you don't know how many times, use as long as. It keeps going while something is yes.
type:

    set guess to 1
    as long as guess times guess falls below 50:
      set guess to guess plus 1
    scrawl "The first square past 50 is ", guess times guess
zoom: lines 8-11
spot: lines 9-10
say:
  As long as guess times guess is below fifty, add one to guess.
  When it's no longer below fifty, the loop stops, and the program carries on below it.
spot: off
zoom: out

say: Two more words steer a loop from inside: move on, and break free.
type:

    for each n from 1 to 10:
      if n is 3: move on
      if n is 6: break free
      scrawl "n is ", n
zoom: lines 12-15
spot: line 13
say: Move on skips the rest of this round, and goes straight to the next one. So three is never shown.
spot: line 14
say: Break free leaves the loop altogether. So at six it stops.
spot: off
run
zoom: output
say: Ten, five, zero. Sixty four is the first square past fifty. And one, two, four, five.
wait
zoom: out

card: Chapter 4 | Every and whenever | Things that happen on their own
say:
  A command can also say what to do every so often, and what to do whenever something happens.
uncard
clear
type:
  genesis:
    set ticks to 0
    every 0.5 seconds:
      set ticks to ticks plus 1
      scrawl "tick [ticks]"
    whenever ticks is 3:
      scrawl "That's three."
      end the program with 0
    scrawl "Waiting..."
  and the universe said i love you
zoom: lines 2-9
spot: lines 3-5
say: Every half a second, add one to ticks, and show it.
spot: lines 6-8
say:
  Whenever ticks is three, say so, and end the program. End the program with zero stops it at once. Zero means all went well.
spot: line 9
say:
  Here's the interesting part. Every and whenever don't start straight away. They start once the command's other lines are done.
  So waiting is shown first.
spot: off
run
zoom: output
say: Waiting, then a tick every half second, and at three, it stops.
wait
zoom: out
say:
  In a game, every frame works the same way, for what happens sixty times a second, and whenever key pressed space, for what a key does.
